    directAnswer: 'An AI receptionist usually costs less than employing a person solely to cover routine calls, but it is not a full substitute for human reception. A fair comparison includes wages, super, leave, equipment, management and coverage on the human side; setup, monthly fees, usage and oversight on the AI side. The strongest trade setup often uses AI for missed and repetitive intake while a person handles judgment, complaints and exceptions.',
    readMinutes: 8, heroImage: '/blog/receptionist-and-tradie-team.webp', heroAlt: 'Australian plumbing receptionist coordinating calls while a field tradie reviews structured alerts',
    secondaryImage: '/app-screens/job-pipeline.png', secondaryAlt: 'TradieRelay action pipeline supporting a human receptionist and trade team', secondaryCaption: 'AI can organise repetitive intake; people still own the decisions, customer relationships and exceptions visible in the pipeline.',
    keyPoints: ['Compare like-for-like hours and responsibilities.', 'Employment cost is more than the hourly rate.', 'AI should remove repetitive interruptions, not erase human judgment.'],
    sections: [
      { heading: 'Start with the actual job to be done', paragraphs: ['If the need is missed-call and after-hours capture, a full-time receptionist is not the only comparison. If the role includes scheduling, supplier coordination, complaints, accounts and customer relationships, AI phone intake covers only one part of it.'] },
      { heading: 'Include the full human employment cost', paragraphs: ['Fair Work says the Clerks Award may cover private-sector employees who answer calls, operate a switchboard or attend reception, depending on the role and workplace. Employers must assess the correct award and include applicable wages, super, leave, penalties, equipment and management—not multiply one headline hourly rate.'] },
      { heading: 'Include the full AI service cost', bullets: ['One-off setup and testing', 'Monthly plan', 'Included and excess usage', 'Carrier forwarding and SMS costs', 'Owner review time', 'Rule changes and support', 'Fallback arrangements'] },
      { heading: 'Value the coverage difference', paragraphs: ['AI can answer simultaneous or after-hours calls without a roster, but it cannot inspect a job, calm every complaint or know the diary changed unless the source is current. Human availability and AI availability solve different parts of the phone problem.'] },
      { heading: 'Use a blended model when staff already exist', paragraphs: ['Let the office person keep high-context customer work. Send missed, overflow and after-hours intake through the assistant, then return structured jobs to the same operating queue.'], callout: { label: 'FAIR COMPARISON', text: 'Compare the AI with the repetitive coverage it replaces—not with every useful thing a good human receptionist does.' } },
    ],
    example: { title: 'An office coordinator stays in control', text: 'Emma handles existing commercial clients, scheduling and complaints. When she is already on a call, Relay captures the next new enquiry and places it in her action pipeline. The business avoids adding a second reception shift while keeping Emma on the work that requires judgment.' },
    productVisual: 'pipeline', productCaption: 'The working pipeline supports a blended workflow: AI creates the organised job; a person chooses what happens next.',
